A MON document holds one record. Each entry gives a field name and a literal value. Here a save file includes a window size, two player names and a score for each player.

[codeblock, $code "text":
    format_version = 1,
    title = "Strategy",
    window = (width = 1280, height = 720),
    names = ["Priya", "Rob"],
    scores = ["Priya" = 10, "Rob" = 12],
    note = none,
]

The `names` sequence contains two Strings. A MON reader never joins or splices
its items. A prepared schema supplies field types and validated defaults for
structs and choice payloads. A missing required field fails. An explicit
`none` stays `none`, even when the field has a default.

You can enclose the root record in parentheses. To store a single scalar, collection or choice as a document, give it a named root field.
Decoding applies defaults only to omitted fields. Encoding emits the completed
record in schema order, including explicit `none` and values equal to
defaults. See @#mon-format (MON format) for the complete literal-data
reference.

Each line marked below illustrates a separate error. The schema for these examples declares `width` as I32 and `scores` as a map with String keys.

[codeblock, $code "text":
    -- INVALID: MON never evaluates expressions, including foldable ones
    width = 1280 + 0,

    -- INVALID: a template is producer syntax, not a MON string literal
    title = [: Strategy],

    -- INVALID: missing comma between entries
    title = "Strategy"
    width = 1280,

    -- INVALID: [] is a collection, never an empty map; use [=]
    scores = [],

    -- INVALID: duplicate keys are rejected, not merged
    scores = ["Priya" = 10, "Priya" = 12],

    -- INVALID: decimal spelling never converts to an I32 target
    width = 1280.0,

    -- INVALID: unknown record fields fail, even with defaults nearby
    titl = "Strategy",

    -- INVALID: reserved user names also fail with underscores or different case
    __LoOp = 1,

    -- INVALID: the field label and `=` must share one logical line
    title
    = "Strategy",
]

Independent Rust callers use `moth_mon`. Compiler clients may use the same API
and Rust types through the convenience re-export `moth::mon`. Prepare a schema
once and reuse it across documents. The codec returns owned values or a
structured `MonError` that supports ordinary Rust reporting and propagation.
The schema declares numeric widths and decimal scales explicitly. MON also retains lossless Integer and Decimal data. It never infers a default numeric type from source. The Rust API uses exact numeric schemas without profile selection or aliases. A raw whole literal can receive an `I64` schema directly, but a
programmatic `Value::I32` cannot satisfy it. Binary-float literals round
directly at their declared precision. See @#mon-format (MON format).
